  "worked_solution": "We write down $C$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $C$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[C\\,|\\,I] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 2 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & \u223c[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{3} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 2 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & \u223c[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{3} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -\\frac{4}{3} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{3} \\\\  & \u223c[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{3} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -\\frac{4}{3} & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding row operations are\n\n$$\n{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${C}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{C}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{4}{3} & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
